#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude-transcript-scan: find credentials that have already landed in a session transcript.

The preventive half of this problem is block-dangerous-bash.sh, which denies a command
before it runs. It cannot close the whole gap, and the reason is structural rather than a
missing rule: no hook can rewrite a tool result. Verified 2026-08-29 against Claude Code
2.1.251 — the PreToolUse output fields are permissionDecision, permissionDecisionReason,
updatedInput, additionalContext and systemMessage, and none of them replaces output.
Claude Code's own redactor (sk-ant-*, Bearer, JWT, gh*_/gl*-/xox*-) is called from the
logger and from redactForDisplay, so it covers the journal and the terminal, not the
transcript the model reads back.

So a `grep` that happens to print a line CARRYING a key is undecidable at the gate: the
command names no secret path and no decrypt verb. Detection is the only layer left, and it
is a bounded problem where prevention is an asymptotic one. This is that layer.

What it does NOT do: un-leak anything. A secret in a transcript stays there. The output of
this script is a rotation trigger — the value is already spent, and the only question left
is how long it stays valid.

  claude-transcript-scan                    # transcripts touched in the last 24h
  claude-transcript-scan --since 72         # ...or a different window, in hours
  claude-transcript-scan --session PATH     # one transcript (what the SessionEnd hook runs)
  claude-transcript-scan --all              # every transcript on the machine
  claude-transcript-scan --accept-baseline  # add this run's findings to the baseline; report only new ones
  claude-transcript-scan --paranoid         # gitleaks' FULL ruleset, for a deliberate audit
  claude-transcript-scan --no-loki          # transcripts only; skip the Loki store

BOTH stores are scanned by default. Claude Code sends the full command text to Loki as
tool_input/tool_parameters and the prompt as `prompt`, and Loki keeps them for 744h, so
a credential typed inline on a command line has two lifetimes. Every verdict names the
stores it covered, because "clean across 37 source(s)" cannot be told apart from a scan
that skipped one. A leak present in both reports twice, under `loki` and under the
transcript's name, and each needs accepting separately -- two copies, two lifetimes.

--no-loki is for a machine with no otelq or no reachable Loki. Without it a missing
otelq is exit 3, could-not-evaluate, the same as a missing gitleaks: skipping a store
silently is the failure this tool exists to prevent.

macOS is the exception: Loki is off there by default, and the verdict says so. The work
Mac has no Loki by design: the org's managed settings send its telemetry, metrics only, to
Lithic's collector, and its local stack was removed on 2026-10-20 (home/claude-otel/
README.md, "The org override on the work Mac"). There is no second store to cover, so
defaulting to Loki only made every SessionEnd scan fail 24 hourly slices. --loki still
turns it back on, for a Mac that runs its own collector.
  claude-transcript-scan --clear-pending    # dismiss the statusline alert WITHOUT baselining anything

Exit 0 clean or nothing new, 1 NEW findings, 2 usage error, 3 could-not-evaluate. 3 is
deliberately not 0: a missing gitleaks means the question was never asked, and a detector
that reports clean when it did not run is worse than no detector. A run where SOME sources
failed is 3 as well, unless the sources that did scan produced a new finding: then it is 1,
because a failed Loki slice must never hide a real leak found elsewhere. Either way the
failed sources are named in the verdict and in the pending marker.

WHY A BASELINE. The first real sweep, 2026-08-29, returned 52 findings across 82
transcripts: 45 of them gitleaks' `generic-api-key`, which fires on a k8s `secretKeyRef:`
field name and on a SOPS key listing as readily as on a credential. A daily unit that goes
red every day on noise gets muted inside a week, and then it caps nothing — the same
failure mode as a monitor nobody trusts. So known findings are accepted once, by hand,
after triage, and only what is NEW alerts.

The risk runs the other way too, which is why accepting is never automatic and never part
of the timer: a blanket --accept-baseline over an untriaged sweep is exactly how a real
leak gets buried. Triage first, accept second.

WHY A NARROWED RULESET. The baseline clears the historical backlog; it does not stop the
drip. Live sessions kept producing fresh `generic-api-key` matches — 4 in the two minutes
after the first baseline was accepted — because that rule matches on entropy alone and a
transcript is full of long high-entropy identifiers that are not credentials. So the daily
run uses ~/.config/gitleaks/transcript-scan.toml, which disables that one rule and keeps
every named, structured rule. The reasoning and the measurements are in that file.

--paranoid restores the full set. The cost of narrowing is real and is named there: it is
the rule that flagged the Sonarr and Radarr admin passwords, so a deliberate audit should
use --paranoid rather than trusting the daily run to have looked everywhere.
"""  # noqa: E501

from __future__ import annotations

import contextlib
import glob
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from decimal import Decimal

PROG = "claude-transcript-scan"

# Loki refuses limit > max_entries_limit_per_query (5000) outright, so a window holding
# more entries than that must be walked in slices. ~620 entries/hour here, so an hourly
# slice sits an order of magnitude under the cap -- and a slice that DOES reach it is
# counted as a failure rather than silently under-scanned, because under-reporting is
# the one failure this tool cannot recover from.
LOKI_LIMIT = 5000
LOKI_QUERY = (
    '{service_name="claude-code"} | event_name=~`tool_result|user_prompt` '
    "| keep tool_input, tool_parameters, prompt"
)
NOTIFY_TITLE = "Credential in a Claude transcript"
OSASCRIPT = (
    "on run argv\n"
    "      display notification (item 2 of argv) with title (item 1 of argv)\n"
    "    end run"
)

# --- jq-compatible JSON handling
# --------------------------------------------------------- The shell version ran every
# extraction through jq, and what it scans is whatever jq printed. These helpers
# reproduce the parts of jq that decide that text: a stream of concatenated values, a
# parse error anywhere failing the whole source, a UTF-8 BOM skipped, invalid UTF-8 and
# a lone low-surrogate escape read as U+FFFD, and a lone high surrogate escape a parse
# error (jq 1.8). jq also tolerates number spellings Python's parser rejects (01, +1,
# .5, nan); a transcript carrying one counts as a failed source here, never as a clean
# one.
_JSON_WS = " \t\n\r"
_SURROGATE_ESCAPE = re.compile(r"\\u[dD][89a-fA-F]")
_LONE_HIGH = re.compile("[\ud800-\udbff]")
_ANY_SURROGATE = re.compile("[\ud800-\udfff]")
# A huge integer literal must not fail the parse: jq reads it, and so must this.
if hasattr(sys, "set_int_max_str_digits"):
    sys.set_int_max_str_digits(0)


class _Bad(Exception):
    """A parse error: jq stops reading at the first one."""


class _JqError(Exception):
    """A runtime error in one input value: jq reports it and moves to the next."""


class _Num:
    """A number kept as its source text, which is what jq 1.8 prints back."""

    __slots__ = ("text",)

    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def render(self):
        return str(Decimal(self.text))


class _Literal(_Num):
    def render(self):
        return self.text


def _constant(name):
    if name == "NaN":
        return None
    sign = "-" if name == "-Infinity" else ""
    return _Literal(f"{sign}1.7976931348623157e+308")


_PLAIN = json.JSONDecoder()
_KEEP_NUMBERS = json.JSONDecoder(
    parse_int=_Num, parse_float=_Num, parse_constant=_constant
)


def _walk(value, keys=False):
    """Yield every string in `value`, pre-order, object keys in document order."""
    stack = [value]
    while stack:
        v = stack.pop()
        if isinstance(v, str):
            yield v
        elif isinstance(v, dict):
            if keys:
                yield from v
            stack.extend(reversed(list(v.values())))
        elif isinstance(v, list):
            stack.extend(reversed(v))


def _values(raw, decoder):
    """Yield each top-level JSON value of `raw`; raise _Bad at the first parse error."""
    text = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    if text.startswith("\ufeff"):
        text = text[1:]
    check = bool(_SURROGATE_ESCAPE.search(text))
    i, n = 0, len(text)
    while True:
        while i < n and text[i] in _JSON_WS:
            i += 1
        if i >= n:
            return
        try:
            value, i = decoder.raw_decode(text, i)
        except (ValueError, RecursionError):
            raise _Bad from None
        if check and any(_LONE_HIGH.search(s) for s in _walk(value, keys=True)):
            raise _Bad
        yield value


def _jq_each(raw, decoder, fn):
    """Run `fn` over every value like jq: (outputs, ok). A parse error stops it."""
    outs, ok = [], True
    try:
        for value in _values(raw, decoder):
            try:
                outs.append(fn(value))
            except _JqError:
                ok = False
    except _Bad:
        ok = False
    return outs, ok


def _strings_text(value):
    """`[.. | strings] | join("\\n")`: every string leaf, whatever the schema."""
    return "\n".join(_walk(value))


def _index(value, key):
    if value is None:
        return None
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return value.get(key)
    raise _JqError


def _iterate(value):
    if isinstance(value, list):
        return list(value)
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return list(value.values())
    raise _JqError


def _loki_text(value):
    """The Loki extraction: three named fields per stream, nulls dropped, joined."""
    parts = []
    for item in _iterate(_index(_index(value, "data"), "result")):
        stream = _index(item, "stream")
        for field in ("tool_input", "tool_parameters", "prompt"):
            got = _index(stream, field)
            if got is not None:
                parts.append(got)
    rendered = []
    for part in parts:
        if isinstance(part, str):
            rendered.append(part)
        elif isinstance(part, bool):
            rendered.append("true" if part else "false")
        elif isinstance(part, _Num):
            rendered.append(part.render())
        else:
            # join cannot add an object or an array to a string.
            raise _JqError
    return "\n".join(rendered)


def _loki_entries(value):
    """The number of entries in `.data.result`."""
    return len(_iterate(_index(_index(value, "data"), "result")))


def _json_str(s):
    """A string as `jq -c` prints it: raw non-ASCII, DEL escaped, bad bytes U+FFFD."""
    s = s.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").decode("utf-8", "replace")
    return json.dumps(s, ensure_ascii=False).replace("\x7f", "\\u007f")


# --- small shell-compat helpers  ------------------------------------------------------
def _now():
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())


def _out(msg=""):
    sys.stdout.write(msg + "\n")


def _err(msg):
    sys.stderr.write(msg + "\n")


def _pad(s, width):
    """`printf %-Ns`: libc pads by bytes, so a non-ASCII name sits narrower than N."""
    return s + " " * max(0, width - len(s.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")))


def _dirname(path):
    return os.path.dirname(path.rstrip("/") or "/") or "."


def _env(name, default):
    return os.environ.get(name) or default


def _read_lines(path):
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        data = fh.read().decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")
    lines = data.split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()
    return lines


def _find_jsonl(top, minutes):
    """`find TOP -name '*.jsonl' -type f [-mmin -MINUTES]`, in sorted directory order.

    find lists a directory in whatever order the filesystem returns it. Sorting makes
    the order of the report, the log and the pending marker repeatable, and no verdict
    depends on it.
    """
    found = []
    cutoff = None if minutes is None else time.time_ns() - minutes * 60 * 10**9

    def wanted(name, st):
        return (
            stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode)
            and name.endswith(".jsonl")
            and (cutoff is None or st.st_mtime_ns > cutoff)
        )

    def walk(directory):
        try:
            with os.scandir(directory) as it:
                entries = sorted(it, key=lambda e: e.name)
        except OSError:
            return
        for entry in entries:
            path = os.path.join(directory, entry.name)
            try:
                st = entry.stat(follow_symlinks=False)
            except OSError:
                continue
            if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
                walk(path)
            elif wanted(entry.name, st):
                found.append(path)

    try:
        st = os.lstat(top)
        if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            # find does not follow a symlink given as the start point, unless it ends in
            # /.
            if not top.endswith("/"):
                return found
            st = os.stat(top)
    except OSError:
        return found
    if stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        walk(top)
    elif wanted(os.path.basename(top), st):
        found.append(top)
    return found


def _lines_bytes(outs):
    """One line per input value, which is what `jq -r` prints, as the bytes scanned."""
    text = "".join(f"{o}\n" for o in outs)
    if _ANY_SURROGATE.search(text):
        text = _ANY_SURROGATE.sub("�", text)
    return text.encode("utf-8")


class Scanner:
    def __init__(self):
        home = os.environ.get("HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~")
        self.home = home
        self.projects = _env("CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_ROOT", f"{home}/.claude/projects")
        self.log = _env(
            "CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_LEAK_LOG", f"{home}/.claude/logs/transcript-leaks.jsonl"
        )
        self.baseline = _env(
            "CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_LEAK_BASELINE",
            f"{home}/.claude/logs/transcript-leaks-baseline.txt",
        )
        self.gl_config = _env(
            "CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_GITLEAKS_CONFIG",
            f"{home}/.config/gitleaks/transcript-scan.toml",
        )
        self.gl_loki_config = _env(
            "CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_GITLEAKS_LOKI_CONFIG",
            f"{home}/.config/gitleaks/transcript-scan-loki.toml",
        )
        # A finding outlives the process that found it, and neither unattended caller
        # keeps the verdict: session-end.sh backgrounds this with its output discarded,
        # and the timer's journal line is read by nobody. A desktop notification does
        # not close that gap either — notify-send needs a running daemon and osascript
        # needs a Mac, and a headless host has neither. So the durable alert is a FILE,
        # which the Claude Code statusline shows to the operator on every platform.
        # Cleared by --accept-baseline or --clear-pending.
        self.pending = _env(
            "CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_LEAK_PENDING",
            f"{home}/.claude/logs/transcript-leaks-pending",
        )
        self.since_h = "24"
        self.mode = "window"
        self.one = ""
        self.accept = False
        self.paranoid = False
        self.clear = False
        # Loki is scanned by DEFAULT. It was opt-in for one day and that was a mistake:
        # --loki lived only in the systemd unit, so a hand-run scan quietly covered one
        # store of two and printed a clean verdict for a store it never opened. "The
        # obvious invocation does less than the timer" is the same
        # could-not-run-reads-as-clean failure this tool exists to prevent. --no-loki is
        # the explicit way out, for a machine with no otelq or no Loki. On macOS the
        # default is off; see the header. CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_SCAN_OS stands in for uname
        # in the tests.
        self.loki = True
        self.loki_skip_why = "--no-loki"
        if _env("CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_SCAN_OS", os.uname().sysname) == "Darwin":
            self.loki = False
            self.loki_skip_why = "no Loki on macOS; --loki to scan it"

        self.gitleaks = ""
        self.found = 0
        self.known = 0
        self.scanned = 0
        self.failed = 0
        # The sources behind `failed`, for the verdict and the pending marker. A count
        # alone says the scan was incomplete without saying which slice or transcript to
        # re-run.
        self.failed_sources = []
        # Findings already reported in THIS run: the overlap between Loki slices hands
        # the same finding back twice, and counting it twice would make the total
        # meaningless. Distinct from the baseline check, which is about findings
        # accepted in an EARLIER run.
        self.seen = set()
        self.baselined = set()
        # This run's unbaselined fingerprints, in order, for the pending marker's dedupe
        # below.
        self.new_fps = []

    # -- arguments
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    def parse(self, argv):
        i = 0
        while i < len(argv):
            arg = argv[i]
            # A flag whose value is missing takes the empty string. The shell version
            # ran `shift 2 || true`, which leaves the flag in place when only one
            # argument remains, so it re-read the same flag forever.
            nxt = argv[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(argv) else ""
            if arg == "--since":
                self.since_h = nxt
                i += 2
            elif arg == "--session":
                self.mode = "one"
                self.one = nxt
                i += 2
            elif arg == "--all":
                self.mode = "all"
                i += 1
            elif arg == "--accept-baseline":
                self.accept = True
                i += 1
            elif arg == "--paranoid":
                self.paranoid = True
                i += 1
            elif arg == "--loki":  # kept: still accepted, now the default
                self.loki = True
                i += 1
            elif arg == "--no-loki":
                self.loki = False
                self.loki_skip_why = "--no-loki"
                i += 1
            elif arg == "--clear-pending":
                self.clear = True
                i += 1
            elif arg in ("-h", "--help"):
                _out("\n".join((__doc__ or "").split("\n")[:56]))
                sys.exit(0)
            else:
                _err(f"{PROG}: unknown argument: {arg}")
                sys.exit(2)

    # -- the pending marker
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------
    def _append(self, path, text):
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError), open(path, "ab") as fh:
            fh.write(text.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape"))

    # Exit 3 is could-not-evaluate, and it is the one verdict with no other durable
    # trace: a finding lands in $LOG, but a scan that never ran leaves nothing behind at
    # all. Record it in the same marker, as a zero-count row, so the statusline reports
    # "the detector could not run" rather than the silence that is indistinguishable
    # from "clean". A partial run also lands here, and the statusline tells it apart by
    # the `failed to scan:` text in its reason, so keep that wording in `partial` below.
    def mark_unavailable(self, why):
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.makedirs(_dirname(self.pending), exist_ok=True)
        self._append(self.pending, f"{_now()}\t0\t{why}\n")

    # Counts one source as not scanned, or not scanned fully. `name` names it in the
    # verdict.
    def note_failed(self, name):
        self.failed += 1
        self.failed_sources.append(name)

    # -- gitleaks
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    def _gitleaks(self, args, data):
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                [self.gitleaks, *args],
                input=data,
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            )
        except OSError:
            return 127, b""
        rc = proc.returncode
        return (128 - rc if rc < 0 else rc), proc.stdout

    def find_gitleaks(self):
        # gitleaks is not installed system-wide here; prek fetches it into its own cache
        # for the repo hook. Take it from PATH first so a real install wins, then the
        # cache, and refuse rather than skip if neither exists.
        gl = os.environ.get("GITLEAKS_BIN") or ""
        if not gl:
            gl = shutil.which("gitleaks") or ""
        if not gl:
            cache = os.path.join(
                glob.escape(self.home), ".cache/prek/hooks/golang-*/bin/gitleaks"
            )
            for candidate in sorted(glob.glob(cache)):
                if os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
                    gl = candidate
                    break
        self.gitleaks = gl

    # gitleaks exits 1 both for "leaks found" and for a ruleset it cannot load, and only
    # the first writes a report. scan_work accepts 1 and counts the report's entries, so
    # a broken ruleset read as a clean scan of every source. A targetRules entry naming
    # a rule that gitleaks no longer ships is enough to break one. So each ruleset is
    # loaded once against empty input before any source is scanned: empty input exits 0,
    # and any other exit there can only come from the config.
    def check_ruleset(self, flags, label):
        args = [
            "stdin",
            "--no-banner",
            *flags,
            "--report-format",
            "json",
            "--report-path",
            "/dev/null",
        ]
        if self._gitleaks(args, b"")[0] == 0:
            return
        _err(
            f"{PROG}: gitleaks could not load the {label} ruleset "
            f"({' '.join(flags) or 'default set'}) — did not scan"
        )
        self.mark_unavailable(f"gitleaks could not load the {label} ruleset")
        sys.exit(3)

    # Both stores scan the same way once their text is in `work`, so the gitleaks call,
    # the fingerprint and the report live here rather than being written twice. `src` is
    # what the log records as the source, `tok` the token that goes in the fingerprint,
    # `rules` the ruleset flags.
    def scan_work(self, src, tok, rules, work):
        # The human column wants something short; `src` is a full path or a slice range.
        # Basename for a transcript, the range itself for Loki -- both already identify
        # the finding, and the JSONL record keeps the unabridged source either way.
        tok_label = src.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        # --redact keeps the finding from repeating the secret into the journal and the
        # log file. A detector that prints what it found is a second copy of the leak.
        args = [
            "stdin",
            "--redact",
            "--no-banner",
            *rules,
            "--report-format",
            "json",
            "--report-path",
            "-",
        ]
        rc, report = self._gitleaks(args, work)
        if rc not in (0, 1):
            _err(f"{PROG}: scan failed (rc={rc}): {src}")
            self.note_failed(src)
            return
        self.scanned += 1
        outs, ok = _jq_each(report, _PLAIN, lambda v: v)
        if not ok or len(outs) != 1 or not isinstance(outs[0], list):
            return
        lines = None
        for item in outs[0]:
            rule, line = self._rule_and_line(item)
            if not rule:
                continue
            if lines is None:
                lines = work.split(b"\n")
            # Fingerprint on CONTENT, not on position. gitleaks' own Fingerprint is
            # `:<rule>:<line>` and the line is an offset into a derived projection of
            # the source, so compacting a transcript or changing that projection
            # renumbers every finding and a position-keyed baseline silently stops
            # matching. Hashing the matched line survives both. The hash never leaves
            # this machine and cannot be reversed into the value.
            number = int(line) if re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", line) else 0
            digest = self._line_hash(lines, number)
            fp = f"{rule}:{tok}:{digest}"
            if fp in self.seen:
                continue
            self.seen.add(fp)
            if fp in self.baselined:
                self.known += 1
                if not self.accept:
                    continue
            if self.accept:
                self._append(self.baseline + ".new", fp + "\n")
                continue
            self.found += 1
            self.new_fps.append(fp)
            # Rule id, line, source, fingerprint and the redacted match. Enough to find
            # it and to name what to rotate, and to accept it later; never enough to
            # re-leak it. Two audiences, two formats. The JSONL record is what
            # --accept-baseline and any later triage read, so it keeps every field;
            # stdout gets a line a person can scan.
            #
            # The match is gitleaks' own `Match` under --redact, which replaces the
            # secret with REDACTED and leaves the framing around it, for example
            # `API_KEY=REDACTED`.
            # The framing is the only record of WHAT fired: the hashed line is gone once
            # the slice ages out, and #847 could not be triaged because a re-fetched
            # slice's line no longer showed what matched. Capped, since a multi-line
            # match such as a private key would otherwise copy its framing in full.
            match = self._redacted_match(item)
            self._append(
                self.log,
                f'{{"ts":{_json_str(_now())},"transcript":{_json_str(src)},"rule":{_json_str(rule)},'
                f'"line":{number},"fingerprint":{_json_str(fp)},"secret":"[redacted]",'
                f'"match":{_json_str(match)}}}\n',
            )
            _out(f"  {_pad(rule, 18)} {_pad(tok_label, 34)} line {line}")

    @staticmethod
    def _rule_and_line(item):
        """Split `RuleID<TAB>StartLine` the way `IFS=$'\\t' read -r rule line` does."""
        if not isinstance(item, dict):
            return "", ""

        def text(v):
            if isinstance(v, str):
                return v
            return json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(",", ":"))

        joined = f"{text(item.get('RuleID'))}\t{text(item.get('StartLine'))}".strip(
            "\t"
        )
        parts = re.split(r"\t+", joined, maxsplit=1)
        return parts[0], parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else ""

    @staticmethod
    def _redacted_match(item):
        """Return gitleaks' redacted `Match` for a report entry, capped at 200 chars.

        Returns "" when the entry has no string `Match`. The caller must have run
        gitleaks with --redact; this does no redaction of its own.
        """
        match = item.get("Match") if isinstance(item, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(match, str):
            return ""
        return match if len(match) <= 200 else match[:200] + "..."

    @staticmethod
    def _line_hash(lines, number):
        """First 16 hex of sha256 of the line `sed -n "${number}p"` prints."""
        last = len(lines) - 1
        # `lines` came from splitting on \n, so its last element is what follows the
        # final newline. sed adds no newline to a final line that lacked one.
        count = last if lines[last] == b"" else last + 1
        data = b""
        if 1 <= number <= count:
            data = lines[number - 1] + (b"\n" if number - 1 < last else b"")
        return hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()[:16]

    # -- the run
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    def run(self, argv):
        sys.stdout.reconfigure(
            encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape", line_buffering=True
        )
        sys.stderr.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape")
        self.parse(argv)

        # The way out that does not also accept anything. Triaging a finding — rotating
        # the credential and confirming it — settles it without adding a fingerprint to
        # the baseline, and with no way to say so the banner would either persist
        # forever or push you toward a blanket accept, which is the one thing this whole
        # tool warns against.
        if self.clear:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.remove(self.pending)
            _out(
                f"{PROG}: cleared the pending-findings marker. "
                f"The log at {self.log} is untouched."
            )
            return 0
        if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+", self.since_h):
            _err(f"{PROG}: --since wants whole hours")
            return 2

        # The daily run uses a narrowed ruleset; --paranoid uses gitleaks' full default
        # set. The reason is in the config file itself — `generic-api-key` matches on
        # entropy alone, and a transcript is full of long high-entropy identifiers that
        # are not credentials.
        #
        # A MISSING config falls back to the full set rather than refusing. That
        # direction is deliberate and is the opposite of the missing-gitleaks case
        # below: no gitleaks means the question was never asked, while no config means
        # it was asked too broadly. Over-reporting is recoverable by reading the
        # findings; under-reporting is not.
        gl_rules = []
        if not self.paranoid:
            if os.path.isfile(self.gl_config):
                gl_rules = ["--config", self.gl_config]
            else:
                _err(
                    f"{PROG}: no ruleset at {self.gl_config} — "
                    "falling back to gitleaks' full set, expect noise"
                )
        # The Loki arm has its own ruleset: the FULL default set (generic-api-key stays
        # live, see the Loki section below for why) plus allowlists for the two
        # public-text shapes that produced 127 of the 247 rows triaged on 2026-09-18. A
        # missing file falls back to the bare default set, which is what this arm ran
        # until then -- noisier, never narrower.
        gl_loki_rules = []
        if not self.paranoid and os.path.isfile(self.gl_loki_config):
            gl_loki_rules = ["--config", self.gl_loki_config]

        self.find_gitleaks()
        if not self.gitleaks or not os.access(self.gitleaks, os.X_OK):
            _err(
                f"{PROG}: no gitleaks binary (PATH, $GITLEAKS_BIN, or the prek cache) "
                "— did not scan"
            )
            self.mark_unavailable("no gitleaks binary")
            return 3
        # A systemd user unit starts with a minimal PATH and otelq lives in
        # ~/.local/bin, so this is a live failure mode rather than a theoretical one.
        # Same rule as gitleaks: refuse loudly, because a missing query tool would
        # otherwise read as a clean Loki store.
        if self.loki and not shutil.which("otelq"):
            _err(
                f"{PROG}: otelq unavailable — did not scan Loki. "
                "Pass --no-loki to scan transcripts only."
            )
            self.mark_unavailable("otelq unavailable")
            return 3
        self.check_ruleset(gl_rules, "transcript")
        if self.loki:
            self.check_ruleset(gl_loki_rules, "loki")

        if self.mode == "one":
            if not os.path.isfile(self.one):
                _err(f"{PROG}: no such transcript: {self.one}")
                return 2
            files = [self.one]
        elif self.mode == "all":
            files = _find_jsonl(self.projects, None)
        else:
            # -mmin rather than -mtime: the window is given in hours and -mtime rounds
            # to days, which would silently widen a 1h sweep to 24.
            files = _find_jsonl(self.projects, int(self.since_h) * 60)

        # An empty transcript list is only a reason to stop when transcripts are the
        # whole job. With --loki there is a second store to scan, and exiting here would
        # have made the Loki arm silently conditional on a transcript having been
        # written in the same window.
        if not files and not self.loki:
            _out(f"{PROG}: no transcripts in scope")
            return 0

        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.makedirs(_dirname(self.log), exist_ok=True)
        if os.path.isfile(self.baseline):
            self.baselined = set(_read_lines(self.baseline))
        # Named in every verdict. "clean across 37 source(s)" cannot be told apart from
        # a scan that skipped a whole store; "clean across 37 source(s) (transcripts)"
        # can.
        stores = "transcripts"

        if self.loki:
            _out(
                f"{PROG}: scanning {len(files)} transcript(s) and "
                f"{self.since_h} hourly Loki slice(s)"
            )
        else:
            _out(
                f"{PROG}: scanning {len(files)} transcript(s); "
                f"Loki skipped ({self.loki_skip_why})"
            )

        # Section headers print unconditionally, before their arm runs. A header with
        # nothing under it is the useful case: it says the store was searched and was
        # clean, which an absent header cannot. The whole reason this output was
        # reshaped is that a clean verdict and a skipped store looked the same.
        _out("\ntranscripts")
        found_before_transcripts = self.found

        for path in files:
            # A transcript deleted since the listing holds nothing to leak, so it is
            # skipped before it can count as a failed source.
            if not os.path.isfile(path):
                continue
            # Every string leaf of every record takes part, which is what makes this
            # independent of the transcript schema: a value moving from .message.content
            # to some new field stays covered without a change here.
            try:
                with open(path, "rb") as fh:
                    raw = fh.read()
                outs, ok = _jq_each(raw, _PLAIN, _strings_text)
            except OSError:
                outs, ok = [], False
            if not ok:
                _err(f"{PROG}: jq could not read {path} — treat as not scanned")
                self.note_failed(path)
                continue
            self.scan_work(path, os.path.basename(path), gl_rules, _lines_bytes(outs))

        # ---- the Loki store
        # --------------------------------------------------------------- Claude Code
        # attaches the full command text to every tool_result as tool_input and
        # tool_parameters, and the prompt to every user_prompt, so a credential typed
        # inline on a command line lands in Loki as well as in the transcript. Loki
        # keeps it for 744h. Nothing scanned there before 2026-08-31.
        #
        # TWO deliberate differences from the transcript arm, and they depend on each
        # other:
        #
        #  * The extraction is NARROW -- three named fields, not every string leaf. A
        #    Loki payload is mostly trace_id/span_id/session_id/user_id: high-entropy
        #    hex, which is exactly what generic-api-key matches on.
        #  * The ruleset is therefore the FULL default set, never the transcript one.
        #    generic-api-key is disabled for the daily transcript run because it
        #    produced 45 false positives there, and it is the only rule that catches an
        #    unstructured `NAME=<32 hex>` -- the shape of every *arr API key. Scanning
        #    Loki with the narrowed ruleset would find the same nothing the transcript
        #    run finds, which is the entire reason this arm exists.
        #
        # Measured 2026-08-31 on this host: the full ruleset over one hour of narrowed
        # Loki text (378KB) returned 0 findings, and over the events holding a real
        # leaked *arr key it returned 12. Narrow the extraction and the expensive rule
        # becomes affordable.
        #
        # The Loki ruleset is that full set plus allowlists, never a narrowing:
        # transcript-scan-loki.toml keeps generic-api-key on and removes two public-text
        # shapes (an SSH key fingerprint, a backtick-quoted kebab identifier in prose)
        # that were re-logged on every run between 2026-09-03 and 2026-09-13. The rule
        # caught the jellyfin key on 2026-09-09 through the same arm, so the allowlists
        # are scoped to the shapes measured, not to the rule.
        found_transcripts = self.found - found_before_transcripts
        if found_transcripts <= 0:
            _out("  none")

        found_loki = 0
        if self.loki:
            _out("\nloki")
            found_before_loki = self.found
            self.scan_loki(gl_loki_rules)
            stores = "transcripts+loki"
            found_loki = self.found - found_before_loki
            if found_loki <= 0:
                _out("  none")

        if self.failed > 0 and self.scanned == 0:
            _err(f"{PROG}: every source failed to scan — treat as not run")
            self.mark_unavailable("every source failed to scan")
            return 3

        # Some sources failed and the rest scanned. Checking `failed` only when
        # `scanned` was 0 let one Loki slice that timed out sit behind a "clean"
        # verdict, with no marker for the statusline to show (dotfiles #832). The daily
        # timer cannot afford that: a slice it misses has left its 25h window by the
        # next run. One marker row per run, written here rather than where each source
        # failed, so the statusline counts one incomplete scan as one.
        failed_names = ", ".join(self.failed_sources)
        partial = ""
        if self.failed > 0:
            partial = f"{self.failed} source(s) failed to scan: {failed_names}"
            _err(f"{PROG}: {partial} — treat those as not scanned")

        if self.accept:
            self.write_baseline()
            # Everything outstanding is now baselined, so the marker has nothing left to
            # report -- except the sources this run never saw, which nothing was
            # accepted for.
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.remove(self.pending)
            if partial:
                self.mark_unavailable(partial)
            try:
                with open(self.baseline, "rb") as fh:
                    held = fh.read().count(b"\n")
            except OSError:
                held = 0
            _out(
                f"{PROG}: accepted this run's findings; "
                f"the baseline in {self.baseline} holds {held} finding(s)"
            )
            _out(
                "Only findings NOT in that file are reported from now on. "
                "Triage before accepting — accepting is how a real leak gets buried."
            )
            return 0

        if partial:
            self.mark_unavailable(partial)

        if self.found > 0:
            return self.report_found(
                found_transcripts, found_loki, partial, failed_names
            )

        # Never "clean" over a source that was not scanned. Nothing turned up in what
        # did scan, but the question was not answered for the rest, which is
        # could-not-evaluate.
        if partial:
            _out(
                f"\nincomplete — nothing new in {self.scanned} source(s) scanned in "
                f"{stores}, {self.known} baselined; {self.failed} failed: "
                f"{failed_names}."
            )
            return 3

        # "nothing new" and "nothing found" are deliberately different sentences.
        # Collapsing them hides whether the baseline is doing any work, and a baseline
        # nobody can see is one nobody trusts — which is how it ends up suppressing
        # something that mattered.
        if self.known > 0:
            _out(
                f"\nno new findings — {self.scanned} source(s) scanned in {stores}, "
                f"{self.known} already baselined."
            )
            return 0

        _out(f"\nclean — {self.scanned} source(s) scanned in {stores}.")
        return 0

    def scan_loki(self, rules):
        # --since is the window WIDTH and --until moves its END, so an hourly slice is
        # `--since <width>`. It read `--since ${h}h` until 2026-08-31 and every slice
        # was therefore a full h-hour window ending ${h-1}h ago -- slice 30 re-scanned
        # the whole 30 hours after it. Two abutting slices returned an identical 4858
        # entries, which is what exposed it, and a 30h scan reported 30x the events Loki
        # actually held.
        #
        # 70m for a 60m step, so consecutive slices OVERLAP by ten minutes. Each query
        # computes `now` when it runs, so a sequence of them drifts forward and
        # exactly-abutting windows leave a sliver uncovered between each pair: measured
        # 4087 of an authoritative 4130 events over 30h before the overlap. Duplicates
        # are free here because the fingerprint is a content hash and `seen` drops a
        # repeat within the run; a gap is not recoverable.
        h = int(self.since_h)
        while h > 0:
            until_h = h - 1
            label = f"{h}h..{until_h}h"
            query = [
                "otelq", "logs", LOKI_QUERY,
                "--stream", "--since", "70m", "--until", f"{until_h}h",
                "--limit", str(LOKI_LIMIT), "--timeout", "60",
            ]  # fmt: skip
            try:
                proc = subprocess.run(
                    query, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL
                )
                slice_raw = proc.stdout
            except OSError:
                slice_raw = b""
            if not slice_raw.rstrip(b"\n"):
                _err(
                    f"{PROG}: Loki slice {label} did not return — treat as not scanned"
                )
                self.note_failed(f"Loki slice {label}")
                h = until_h
                continue
            counts, counted = _jq_each(slice_raw, _KEEP_NUMBERS, _loki_entries)
            lines = [str(c) for c in counts] + ([] if counted else ["0"])
            # A multi-line count made the shell's integer test fail, which reads as
            # under the cap.
            if len(lines) == 1 and int(lines[0]) >= LOKI_LIMIT:
                _err(
                    f"{PROG}: Loki slice {label} hit the {LOKI_LIMIT}-entry cap "
                    "— scanned PARTIALLY"
                )
                self.note_failed(
                    f"Loki slice {label} (truncated at {LOKI_LIMIT} entries)"
                )
            outs, ok = _jq_each(slice_raw, _KEEP_NUMBERS, _loki_text)
            if not ok:
                _err(
                    f"{PROG}: jq could not read Loki slice {label} "
                    "— treat as not scanned"
                )
                self.note_failed(f"Loki slice {label}")
                h = until_h
                continue
            # The fingerprint token is a constant, never the slice bounds: a re-scan
            # with different boundaries must produce the same fingerprint or the
            # baseline stops matching. A leak present in both stores therefore reports
            # twice, under `loki` and under the transcript name, and needs acknowledging
            # twice -- which is correct, since they are two copies with two lifetimes.
            self.scan_work(f"{label} ago", "loki", rules, _lines_bytes(outs))
            h = until_h

    def write_baseline(self):
        new = self.baseline + ".new"
        try:
            lines = _read_lines(new)
        except OSError:
            lines = None
        # An accept adds to the baseline and never removes from it. This run's window
        # sees only part of what was accepted before, and a hand-appended entry may
        # never have been in any window, so replacing the file with this run's
        # findings dropped both (dotfiles #849). Re-read at write time rather than
        # reusing the set loaded at start, so an entry appended while the scan ran
        # survives too.
        try:
            kept = _read_lines(self.baseline)
        except OSError:
            kept = []
        merged = set(kept) | set(lines or [])
        merged.discard("")
        try:
            with open(self.baseline, "wb") as fh:
                body = "".join(f"{fp}\n" for fp in sorted(merged))
                fh.write(body.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape"))
        except OSError:
            return
        if lines is not None:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.remove(new)

    def report_found(self, found_transcripts, found_loki, partial, failed_names):
        # Per-store counts, because "18 findings" does not tell you which store to go
        # and read, and the two have different remedies: a transcript is a local file, a
        # Loki record is queryable by anything on the LAN for 744h.
        split = f"{found_transcripts} in transcripts"
        if self.loki:
            split += f", {found_loki} in loki"
        _out(
            f"\n{self.found} new finding(s) — {split}. "
            f"{self.known} baselined, {self.scanned} source(s) scanned."
        )
        if partial:
            _out(f"  Not scanned: {failed_names}")
        _out(f"  Full records: {self.log}")
        _err(
            "  Rotate the named credential: "
            "uv run python scripts/secrets_mgmt/secret_rotation.py rotate <name>"
        )

        # One finding, one count. Loki is machine-wide, so every session's SessionEnd
        # scan re-finds the same unbaselined event inside its window, and a row per scan
        # made the statusline sum one leak into "3 transcript leak(s)" (dotfiles #785).
        # So each row carries its fingerprints in a fourth column, and a run records
        # only the ones no row already holds. The statusline still sums column 2, which
        # is now a distinct count.
        held = set()
        if os.path.isfile(self.pending):
            for row in _read_lines(self.pending):
                cols = row.split("\t")
                if len(cols) >= 4:
                    held.update(cols[3].split(","))
        fresh, dup = [], set()
        for fp in self.new_fps:
            if fp not in held and fp not in dup:
                dup.add(fp)
                fresh.append(fp)
        if not fresh:
            _out(
                f"  Already in the pending marker from an earlier scan: {self.pending}"
            )
            return 1
        joined = ",".join(fresh)
        fresh_n = len([f for f in joined.split(",") if f])

        # The durable half. Written before either banner is attempted, so a machine with
        # no notification path at all still records the finding rather than losing it.
        self._append(self.pending, f"{_now()}\t{fresh_n}\t{self.log}\t{joined}\n")

        # The immediate half, best-effort. Same platform order as hooks/notify.sh:
        # osascript is the Mac's only working path, notify-send the Linux desktop's, and
        # a headless host matches neither and is covered by the file above.
        body = f"{self.found} new finding(s). See {self.log}."
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            if shutil.which("osascript"):
                subprocess.run(
                    ["osascript", "-e", OSASCRIPT, "--", NOTIFY_TITLE, body],
                    stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                    stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
            elif shutil.which("notify-send"):
                subprocess.run(
                    ["notify-send", "-u", "critical", NOTIFY_TITLE, body],
                    stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(Scanner().run(sys.argv[1:]))
